9.4  ACLs and capabilities

TL;DR
  - An ACL gives rights to more people than owner, group and others: "bob may
    read this file too".
  - A + after the rights in ls -l means the file has an ACL.
  - Capabilities split root's power into pieces: a program can get one, such as
    listening on ports under 1024, without running as root.

      setfacl -m u:bob:r report.pdf
      getfacl report.pdf
      sudo setcap cap_net_bind_service=+ep ./server

Pitfalls
  - On a file with an ACL, the group rights ls -l shows are the ACL's mask: a
    chmod g-w there also takes write access away from every named user.
  - A default ACL on a folder (setfacl -d -m …) applies to files created later,
    not to existing ones: add -R for those.
  - Copies drop ACLs unless asked: cp -a, tar --acls, rsync -A.
  - A file's capabilities are lost when the file is replaced, by a rebuild or an
    update: set them again.

Deep dive

  ▾ How does an ACL work?

    It adds entries next to the classic three: named users, named groups, and a
    mask that caps what they and the owning group can get. The kernel checks the
    owner first, then named users, then groups, then others. ext4, btrfs and XFS
    support ACLs without any special setting.

  ▾ Which capabilities exist?

    About forty. CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE opens ports under 1024, CAP_NET_ADMIN
    changes network settings, CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE ignores file rights, and
    CAP_SYS_ADMIN is a catch-all close to full root. man 7 capabilities lists
    them all; grep Cap /proc/self/status shows the sets of a process, as hex
    numbers that capsh --decode translates.

Related
  Commands  getfacl, setfacl, getcap, setcap
  Hub       idk permissions Permissions
  See also  idk 9.1 rwx, octal and umask
